Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 12:00:00 -0500
Subject: kcc: gate the 1.25 s Esc pointer-lock re-lock cooldown

Stock behaviour
(chrome/browser/ui/exclusive_access/pointer_lock_controller.cc):
PointerLockController::HandleUserPressedEscape() stamps
last_user_escape_time_ when the user presses Esc while the pointer is
locked, and PointerLockController::RequestToLockPointer() then refuses
any lock request that arrives within
kEffectiveUserEscapeDuration = base::Milliseconds(1250) of that stamp:

    if (base::TimeTicks::Now() <
        last_user_escape_time_ + kEffectiveUserEscapeDuration) {
      web_contents->GotResponseToPointerLockRequest(
          blink::mojom::PointerLockResult::kUserEscapeCooldown);

unless the previous unlock came from the page itself
(last_unlocked_by_target, i.e. document.exitPointerLock()).

Electron compiles and uses this controller: chromium_src/BUILD.gn lists
//chrome/browser/ui/exclusive_access/pointer_lock_controller.cc, and
shell/browser/api/electron_api_web_contents.cc owns an
ExclusiveAccessManager (exclusive_access_manager_{this}). Esc reaches
HandleUserPressedEscape() through WebContents::PreHandleKeyboardEvent()
-> ExclusiveAccessManager::HandleUserKeyEvent(), and a page lock request
reaches RequestToLockPointer() through WebContents::RequestPointerLock()
-> permission helper -> WebContents::OnRequestPointerLock(). After a
user Esc RenderWidgetHostImpl::is_last_unlocked_by_target_ is false, so
the cooldown branch is the one taken.

For the game this means that after Esc (menu), clicking back into the
game within 1.25 s is rejected: requestPointerLock() fails and mouse
look stays dead until the player clicks again after the window expires.

What the patch changes: with the feature enabled the cooldown refusal is
skipped, nothing else. The user-gesture requirement in the same
function (kRequiresUserGesture), the permission request in Electron, the
"already locked"/focus/sandbox checks in content, the fullscreen
coupling and Blink's own request rate limiter
(RateLimitPointerLockRequests in
third_party/blink/renderer/core/page/pointer_lock_controller.cc) are all
untouched. last_user_escape_time_ is still recorded.

SECURITY NOTE: the cooldown is an anti-trap protection for general
browsing. It guarantees that after the user escapes a pointer lock, a
hostile page cannot grab the pointer back on the user's very next click
or key press, which gives the user time to move the (now visible)
cursor out of the page. Removing it is NOT acceptable for a browser that
shows arbitrary sites. It is acceptable here only because the product
is a single-site game client: the only content that can request pointer
lock is the game the user launched the client to play, there is no
other tab or browser UI to be kept away from, Esc still always releases
the lock, a re-lock still needs a fresh user gesture, and the user can
still leave with the OS (Alt+Tab, close the window). The feature is
process-wide, so do not enable it in a build that also loads untrusted
third-party pages in windows that can take pointer lock.

Gating: base::Feature KccNoPointerLockEscCooldown, disabled by default,
defined in the anonymous namespace of pointer_lock_controller.cc. With
the feature off the condition evaluates exactly the stock expression.
Not a hot path (once per lock request), so the feature is read at the
call.

A/B: --enable-features=KccNoPointerLockEscCooldown. Lock the pointer in
the game, press Esc, click back into the game within about one second.
Off: the first click does not re-lock (the promise rejects; Blink
reports the kUserEscapeCooldown result as a DOMException) and a second
click after ~1.25 s does. On: the first click re-locks. Also confirm
that a requestPointerLock() without a user gesture still fails.

Platforms: all (browser process, UI thread, platform-independent).
Not compiled. base/feature_list.h is newly included in this file.

diff --git a/chrome/browser/ui/exclusive_access/pointer_lock_controller.cc b/chrome/browser/ui/exclusive_access/pointer_lock_controller.cc
index eebf155451f560b498b18bb13713fedb32194675..963113c3fb8a4a4159feb888dcd015de2c9610f5 100644
--- a/chrome/browser/ui/exclusive_access/pointer_lock_controller.cc
+++ b/chrome/browser/ui/exclusive_access/pointer_lock_controller.cc
@@ -4,6 +4,7 @@
 
 #include "chrome/browser/ui/exclusive_access/pointer_lock_controller.h"
 
+#include "base/feature_list.h"
 #include "base/functional/bind.h"
 #include "chrome/browser/ui/exclusive_access/exclusive_access_context.h"
 #include "chrome/browser/ui/exclusive_access/exclusive_access_manager.h"
@@ -26,6 +27,12 @@ namespace {
 constexpr base::TimeDelta kEffectiveUserEscapeDuration =
     base::Milliseconds(1250);
 
+// KCC: when enabled, a pointer lock request that arrives within
+// |kEffectiveUserEscapeDuration| of the user pressing Esc is no longer refused
+// with kUserEscapeCooldown. Every other check is unchanged. The cooldown is an
+// anti-trap protection, so this is only meant for a single-site game client.
+BASE_FEATURE(kKccNoPointerLockEscCooldown, base::FEATURE_DISABLED_BY_DEFAULT);
+
 }  // namespace
 
 PointerLockController::PointerLockController(ExclusiveAccessManager* manager)
@@ -63,8 +70,9 @@ void PointerLockController::RequestToLockPointer(WebContents* web_contents,
       }
       return;
     }
-    if (base::TimeTicks::Now() <
-        last_user_escape_time_ + kEffectiveUserEscapeDuration) {
+    if (!base::FeatureList::IsEnabled(kKccNoPointerLockEscCooldown) &&
+        base::TimeTicks::Now() <
+            last_user_escape_time_ + kEffectiveUserEscapeDuration) {
       web_contents->GotResponseToPointerLockRequest(
           blink::mojom::PointerLockResult::kUserEscapeCooldown);
       if (lock_state_callback_for_test_) {
